\title{Profile Form: A Rigidity Law for the Positional Hit Profile\\ and the Structure of its Beyond-Background Residual}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{25 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{positional hit profile} of a factor-search experiment on semiprimes: the density $T(x)$ of recorded hits as a function of a rescaled offset $x$ in a window. Empirically, over $9594$ hits collected from $128$ semiprimes of bit-length $96$, the profile is described by the power law $T(x)\approx 0.0295\,(1+x)^{-1.104}$, with a resampling interval $[0.991,1.218]$ for the exponent and an Akaike weight of $0.987$ against exponential, logistic and linear rivals. We isolate the mathematics behind that verdict. First, a rigidity theorem: every positive continuous profile that is multiplicative for the scale composition $x\star y=(1+x)(1+y)-1$ is a power law $T(0)(1+x)^{-b}$, with identifiable exponent. Second, a single invariant --- the log-midpoint defect --- separates the power law from the exponential, logistic and affine families simultaneously. Third, the total window mass $\int_0^X(1+x)^{-b}dx$ converges iff $b>1$, and the measured interval straddles that threshold. Fourth, the uniform scale-mixture background $M(x)=(1-e^{-x})/x$ satisfies $1/(2x)\le M(x)\le 1/x$ for $x\ge1$, whence the residual $T/M$ declines by at most two thirds of the raw decline, uniformly in amplitude and exponent. Fifth, the endpoint-pinned concave fits of the residual are peaked exactly when their curvature is below $-1/10$, and the reported curvature interval $[-0.62,-0.14]$ lies entirely on the peaked side; a peaked residual certifies that the background is not a power-law rescaling of the profile. Finally, we show that this certification cannot be strengthened: a two-atom positive mixture yields a peaked residual, the uniform background itself humps near $x=10$ at the measured exponent, the hump location obeys the exact law $x^\star=1/(b-1)$ for $b>1$, and for every $b\ge 3/2$ the residual is strictly decreasing on $(0,\infty)$ --- bracketing a critical exponent in $(1.1,1.5)$ that the measurement also straddles.
\end{abstract}

